					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tombones" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(398766); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=398766&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608067520" alt="tombones" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=398766)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tombones" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(398766); return false;" >tombones</a></span> <span class="odate time_1279056384 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Jul 2010 21:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-828328">
									

<p>I know SCPs are supposed to stand up on their own, but it there are quite a few articles and stories that put 148 to good use (the tragic long-range empathy girl is a perfect example)</p>
<p>It's something that makes the Foundation's continued existence and basic operations seem a little more plausible. In addition to the hundreds of weird and awful horrors they keep locked up, they also have a few useful (but not TOO useful) tools to deal with otherwise insurmountable challenges.</p>
<p>It's also a "success story"- an anomalous object with an actual use, supplying a motive for keeping all the other dubiously useful stuff around in case they've got some terribly helpful properties waiting to be discovered.</p>
<p>This and the healing pills would definitely be overkill if they weren't in limited supply. As they are, they offer a little variety to the series. Of all the SCPs to cross-reference in another article, 148 is one of the better ones.. provided it isn't abused (the memetic tiger kitten requiring "armor made of 148" in the containment procedures springs to mind)</p>

							</div>

								
