					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/leicontis" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(393373); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=393373&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608067520" alt="Leicontis" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=393373)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/leicontis" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(393373); return false;" >Leicontis</a></span> <span class="odate time_1298049756 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Feb 2011 17:22</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1005846">
									

<p>So, a thought to remove some of the annoyingly high utility: Make 148 ablative. Maybe for just screening out "background noise" (i.e. shielding against a receptive telepath being able to sense thoughts), it works as described, but against more serious stuff like <a href="/scp-035">SCP-035</a> or the grinding from <a href="/scp-882">SCP-882</a>, the Telekill gradually erodes or degrades. Alternatively, even that "background noise" degrades it, just more slowly due to the lower intensity. Thus, the already limited quantity is gradually getting smaller, and there's plenty of incentive to work out containment methods that don't require it.</p>
<p>As an alternative, this property could be used to (in effect) non-comically decommission/-ARC 148, by explaining that the degradation wasn't discovered until most of the stock had already been compromised, and what was left got used up during the transition to non-148-dependent containment protocols.</p>

							</div>

								
