		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,1006009)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-1006009">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=587467&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608067520" alt="Adam Smascher" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=587467)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;" >Adam Smascher</a></span> <span class="odate time_1298066102 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Feb 2011 21:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1006009">
									

<p>I like the idea of it being less effective and ablated by everything. The ablative property makes it far more interesting to me; you've got a tangible solid being eroded by something ethereal. I also like the idea of it not being so damn effective.</p>
<p>That said, I think the Foundation would <em>very quickly</em> take note of such a "valued" resource sublimating into nonexistence, which makes the decom/ARC scenario a stretch for me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any personnel involved in unauthorized tests with SCP-148 are to be terminated.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>If you wish to make use of SCP-148, please contact the senior staff member in charge of your division, and they will decide whether to pass the request up the line. The next person to attempt to ask me directly is being reassigned to Kondraki. O5-6</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Can probably guess what I'm going to say about those bits, so I won't bother.</p>
<p>As an alternative to simply sublimating with use, 148 could "soak" it like a sponge soaks water. It doesn't work perfectly, but it works ok for a while. Eventually, though, your telekill's no longer telekill but a big block o' schizo with reversed properties. That's not to say it strengthens weird mind shit, but perhaps manifests cognitohazardous properties, causing individuals exposed to experience a myriad of hallucinations and perhaps symptoms in line with schizophrenia which subside following removal from the vicinity of the "spent" telekill. This would require the "spent" telekill be contained, kinda like radioactive waste.</p>
<p>Additionally, <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breakdown_voltage">an electric approach</a> to the inhibition of outside influences may be interesting.</p>
<p>Other thoughts: May be an improvement if exposure/encapsulation causes cumulative effects such as feelings of <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derealization">derealization</a> and <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Depersonalization">depersonalization</a> which slowly increase in severity with greater exposure, suggesting a person being cut off from the "background noise" isn't a good thing.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-13356-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-13356-1')">1</a></sup> After extended isolation from the "background noise", rapid reintroduction could have its own adverse effects. Additionally, the "background noise" may be what drowns out more malicious influences, such as the aforementioned SCP-882 and SCP-035; thus, telekill <em>increases</em> susceptibility to the more dangerous and powerful weird mind shit by filtering the mostly harmless.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-13356-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-13356-2')">2</a></sup></p>
<p>This probably doesn't make a whole lot of sense. If this is the case, let me know and I shall endeavor to explain.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-13356-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-13356-1')">1</a>. I wouldn't <em>state</em> this though, or even suggest it's suspected. Speculation has no place in a scientific report. Let the reader figure it out.</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-13356-2"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-13356-2')">2</a>. Think of it like light pollution, if the light from the city was largely harmless and the stars drove you batshit.</div>
</div>

